WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.760
"Two and a Half Men" was one of the biggest&nbsp;
hits in sitcom history. But the series finale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:10.260
was missing one of its biggest stars&nbsp;
for a very specific and unique reason:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.480
he didn't want to be crushed to death by a piano.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.720
So what did Charlie Sheen want instead? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:19.260
to understand the power struggle that led&nbsp;
to Sheen's absence from the series finale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:23.460
first you have to understand the&nbsp;
decade of craziness that preceded it.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.740
When the show debuted on CBS in 2003,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:30.720
it was an instant hit. Both Sheen&nbsp;
and co-star Jon Cryer were 80s icons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:35.880
reinventing themselves for a new millennium.&nbsp;
And it worked. Cryer told Entertainment Weekly

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:40.500
"Charlie and I really hit it off. We&nbsp;
had a great first few years on that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:45.360
show. It was incredibly smooth, we had a&nbsp;
lovely time, it was working really well."

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:52.020
And then it suddenly stopped working well,&nbsp;
as Sheen famously and spectacularly went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:56.460
off the rails. The tipping point was&nbsp;
reportedly Sheen's 2005 divorce from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:00.420
Denise Richards. According to Cryer,&nbsp;
that's when Sheen turned to drugs.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.920
"At first he could handle it, and he was still&nbsp;
incredibly professional — and still lovely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:09.420
by the way, to everybody on set — but you&nbsp;
could just see that stuff was wearing on him."

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:12.720
It was just the kind of trouble&nbsp;
the show had been hoping to avoid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.780
as Sheen had been a tabloid regular in the 1990s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:19.140
"Two and a Half Men" producer Chuck&nbsp;
Lorre took a chance on Sheen anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:24.240
writing the role of Charlie Harper for Sheen.&nbsp;
But following his divorce from Richards, Sheen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:29.400
began making headlines again for all the wrong&nbsp;
reasons. He was accused of hiring sex workers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:34.380
arrested on assault charges, and sentenced to&nbsp;
rehab and anger management among other things.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:39.780
Finally, in 2011, during Season 8 of "Two&nbsp;
and a Half Men," the show went on hiatus so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:43.740
that Sheen could deal with his problems.&nbsp;
Instead, he refused to enter a facility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:49.200
fired his private sobriety coach, and&nbsp;
verbally attacked Lorre. That was the end:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:54.840
Lorre fired him, and Sheen responded with a&nbsp;
typically unhinged statement, telling TMZ,

00:01:54.840 --> 00:02:00.420
"This is very good news. They continue to&nbsp;
be in breach, like so many whales. It is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:05.700
big day of gladness at the Sober Valley Lodge&nbsp;
because now I can take all of the bazillions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:09.480
never have to look at [them] again&nbsp;
and I never have to put on those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:14.460
silly shirts for as long as this warlock&nbsp;
exists in the terrestrial dimension."

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:18.780
Sheen wasn't exactly contrite about his drug&nbsp;
fueled antics in later interviews either.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:22.260
"I don't know, man, I was banging&nbsp;
seven gram rocks and finishing them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:25.200
because that's how I roll. I have&nbsp;
one speed, I have one gear: GO!"

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.020
"Two and a Half Men" survived Sheen's departure by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:31.980
killing off Charlie Harper and replacing&nbsp;
him with new character Walden Schmidt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:36.480
played by Ashton Kutcher. The show ran&nbsp;
several more seasons before ending in 2015.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.460
Which is where the piano comes in.

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:43.140
Even though Charlie Harper was dead, producers&nbsp;
hoped to bring back Sheen for the series finale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:46.920
The idea was that Charlie would ring the&nbsp;
doorbell, then rant to the camera about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:51.420
drug abuse and how he was above all that.&nbsp;
Then, a piano would fall from the sky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:55.980
killing him. It would be very meta,&nbsp;
and thus fit the humor of the show.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:01.320
However, Sheen had something totally different&nbsp;
in mind. He was willing to appear in the finale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:05.100
but he wanted his character's return to&nbsp;
be a launching pad for a proposed new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:08.700
spinoff series called "The Harpers,"&nbsp;
which would have reunited him with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:13.080
Cryer. When the producers didn't go&nbsp;
for his idea, he refused to appear.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:18.180
So instead, the producers simply ran the scene&nbsp;
anyway, only with a body double for the absent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:22.860
Sheen. And they added a coda where Lorre&nbsp;
poked fun at both Sheen and himself as well.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:23.640
"Winning."

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:32.340
It was a weird end to Sheen's tenure on "Two&nbsp;
and a Half Men," but not the end of his career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:37.620
or his personal growth. In 2021 he told Yahoo!&nbsp;
Entertainment that he regretted how things went.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:44.280
"There was 55 different ways for me to handle&nbsp;
that situation, and I chose number 56. And so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:48.780
you know, I think the growth for me post-meltdown&nbsp;
or melt forward or melt somewhere — however&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:53.580
you want to label it — it has to start with&nbsp;
absolute ownership of my role in all of it."

